"""
V12.5 neutral geometry/contact analysis for the Fe/O/chiral-ligand + substrate system.

python contact_geometry_v12_5_exact_screenshot_eda.py \
    --structures con_S step_R \
    --eda EDA.csv \
    --cat-ref cat.xyz \
    --sub-ref styrene.xyz

The main change from V5 is SEMANTIC, not merely cosmetic:

1) A negative vdW gap is called a geometric vdW overlap, NOT a steric penalty.
   vdW overlap does not by itself imply favorable or unfavorable interaction energy.

2) Contacts between the reactive O atoms and substrate atoms, after excluding the
   two assigned primary forming C-O pairs, are called SECONDARY O contacts.
   "Secondary" only means "not one of the two assigned primary C-O pairs".
   It does NOT mean nonbonding, repulsive, or unimportant.

3) Secondary O contacts are split by substrate element (O...H, O...C, O...N,
   and O...other) and are also written as a long contact table for inspection.

4) "heavy" naming is made explicit:
      subheavy_ligall = substrate heavy atoms vs all ligand atoms
      suball_ligall   = all substrate atoms vs all ligand atoms

5) The old mapping_geometry_RMS_A name is replaced by
   mapping_distance_matrix_RMS_A because the score is based on internal
   pair-distance differences, not a Kabsch coordinate RMSD.

6) Group-level vdW-overlap sums are accompanied by per-substrate-atom values,
   because symmetry groups have different sizes.

7) The O-C-C-O torsion is kept in three forms:
      signed angle
      absolute angle
      twist from coplanarity = min(|phi|, 180-|phi|)
   The last one intentionally discards syn/anti information.

System indexing (edit if needed):
  1       Fe
  2-62    chiral ligand
  63-64   reactive O atoms
  65-end  substrate

IMPORTANT CHEMICAL CAVEAT
-------------------------
The contact descriptors in this script are GEOMETRIC descriptors only.
A short O...N, O...H, O...C, or ligand...substrate contact may be attractive,
repulsive, partially bonding, dispersive, electrostatic, or mixed. Assigning
its energetic sign requires electronic-structure analysis (EDA/NBO/NCI/IGMH,
etc.), not the vdW-gap criterion itself.
"""

